[Music]  ford  the company that pioneered the modern  car the assembly line the hour work week  and the creator of america's  best-selling vehicle the f-150 despite  this impressive and long-lasting legacy  ford is currently in a rather dire  situation they've got over 200 billion  dollars worth of liabilities which puts  them in the top 10 list of most indebted  companies  while ford doesn't come in at the number  one spot something that we should note  is that all the other companies on this  list generally have a market cap that's  several times larger than ford so while  ford may not be the most indebted  company in the world they're usually the  most leveraged company in the world  thankfully they do have more assets than  liabilities given that their assets come  in at 257 billion dollars  but when we compare their cash reserves  to their liabilities it doesn't look  nearly as pretty ford currently only has  20 billion dollars worth of cash and  cash equivalents in other words their  total liabilities is 10 times their  current cash reserves considering this i  don't think you'd be surprised here that  macroaccess gives the company a 44  chance of bankruptcy now it should be  noted that ford is the only american  legacy automaker that has never filed  for bankruptcy in the past but this may  actually come back to haunt them because  they never got a reset switch like the  other automakers and this is one of the  main reasons that they still have insane  dead levels so is it just a matter of  time until ford goes bankrupt  taking a look back ford's liquidity  problems stayed way further than just  the pandemic or even the 2008 financial  recession in fact the route to ford's  financial problems can be traced back to  the late 1990s when a man named jack  nasser was appointed as the new ceo  jack was an entrepreneur at heart in  fact he had been running businesses  since he was a teenager he had a small  bicycle making operation and he ran  multiple disco clubs so it was only  natural that he brought his  entrepreneurial spirit into ford jack  didn't just come in as an executive  though he actually joined as a modest  financial analyst at age 20 in 1968 and  it was until 31 years later in 1999 that  jack would become ceo  in the meantime he had made a name for  himself as a shrewd businessman many of  his colleagues actually nicknamed him  jack the knife and blackjack jack the  knife was referenced to how many jobs he  had cut over the years and blackjack was  referenced to his intoleration for  unprofitable sectors while these are by  no means the kindest of trades many  successful ceos share these traits so  this in itself did not taint jack's  tenure what tainted his tenure and  screwed over ford financially was his  business decisions themselves first of  all jack tried to transform ford into  some sort of lifestyle company he wanted  people to have a high opinion of ford so  he started buying out luxury competitors  two of these acquisitions included land  rover for 3.3 billion dollars and volvo  for 6.47 billion dollars not only were  these expensive purchases but it also  led the ford having to split their  attention between the companies which  led to all the brands suffering aside  from trying to make forward into a  lifestyle company jack also placed a  massive emphasis on the internet being  an entrepreneur at heart it was obvious  to jack that the internet was the next  big thing and he strongly thought that  the internet should play a significant  role at ford so he spent loads of money  developing for its website affiliate  marketing it dealer portals and so on  these efforts were actually pretty  successful given that ford garnered  millions of visitors every single month  looking back these were pretty visionary  moves but jack missed the mark in two  areas first of all he was diverting so  much attention into the internet that it  was taking away from ford's core  business which was making it good cars  while having a good online presence is  definitely important as a car company  your number one focus should be making  the best cars you can and this was  unfortunately no longer the case with  ford as for jack's second shortfall his  timing was terrible  not only was jack too early for his time  but he was doing all of this right  before the dot-com bubble burst so when  internet companies crashed 90 to 95  percent in the early 2000s jack looked  like an absolute idiot and to a certain  degree he was he had completely  dismantled ford's focus on creating cars  while also burning billions of  acquisitions and the internet hearing  all this i don't think you'd be  surprised here that ford would replace  jack in late 2001 but the damage had  already been done  while jack was out of the company fort  spain had just begun first of all the  acquisitions that jack had completed  weren't performing that well they were  actually performing terribly but wait a  minute land rover and volvo were only a  couple billion each so how much could  this possibly hurt ford  well while the companies themselves  weren't all that expensive for ford  standards the same could not be said  about their balance sheets and cash flow  you see when you acquire a company you  don't just acquire the good parts like  the brand image and the customers you  also acquire the bad parts like their  dead and negative cash flow it's  estimated that holding jaguar and land  rover cost 4 35 to 50 billion dollars a  similar story can be seen with volvo as  well not only were these companies a  massive drag on forced income statements  while they owned them but ford would  also lose a lot of money trying to  offload these companies for example ford  would end up selling volvo for just 1.5  billion dollars as a reminder they paid  6.47 billion dollars to acquire the  company similarly they would end up  selling both jaguar and land rover for  2.3 billion dollars just nine years  before this ford paid 3.3 billion  dollars for land rover alone  clearly these were massive losses for  ford but this was just the beginning of  their problems you see oil prices would  reach nearly 200 per barrel in mid-2008  for perspective if you thought oil  prices were high a couple of months ago  these prices were 60 higher than that so  i don't think you'd be surprised here  that people shied away from buying large  pickup trucks and suvs given that they  would lose a fortune just paying for gas  and the given that large pickup trucks  and suvs were for specialty they were  basically screwed to make things even  worse the us was facing a serious  recession in 2008 which simply led to  four sales declining even more  all of this would climax with ford  losing a record of 14.6 billion dollars  in 2008 and at this point it was only a  matter of time until the government  stepped in and bailed out the company  ford would end up receiving a 5.9  billion loan from the department of  energy which allowed them to narrowly  avoid bankruptcy and start focusing on  developing energy efficient vehicles but  unfortunately for ford this was terrible  timing as well you see oil prices would  crash hard over the next couple of years  which meant that americans could once  again indulge in their love for trucks  and suvs given that ford was preoccupied  with cars like the ford focus this gave  gm the perfect opportunity to make  strides with their tahoe suburban and  escalade series and before you knew it  ford was falling behind in the same  industry that they had dominated for  decades so while ford was the only  legacy american automaker to avoid  bankruptcy it's not like things turned  out better for them anyway  over the past several years ford has  shifted their focus back onto what  they're good at trucks and suvs  they've even gone ahead and announced  that they're going to completely retire  the ford focus in 2025 it seems that  this approach has worked out pretty well  for forward given that they've been able  to make the f-150 the number one selling  vehicle once again  but it's not all good news ford has  never been able to work out the death  mess that they got into in the 2000s  sometimes their liabilities stand at 150  billion dollars and sometimes their  liabilities stand at 250 billion dollars  but for the most part ford hasn't been  able to reduce their average liabilities  to under 200 billion dollars to make  things worse the automotive industry has  been facing a pretty big crossroad over  the past couple of years with evs  fortunately ford has not been ignoring  evs like toyota forward has actually  been taking ev's extremely seriously and  they were the first legacy automaker to  convert a popular gas model to fully  electric so they're definitely on the  right track but this doesn't mean that  the path forward will be easy profitably  scaling ev production is extremely  challenging and ford is experiencing  this firsthand as we speak take the mock  e for example ford started maki  production in q3 of 2020 and they were  able to rent production for three  quarters but ever since then they've not  only been stagnating but declining when  you put the monkeys production ramp next  to the model 3's production ramp you can  really start to see how far behind ford  really is by the seventh quarter of  production tesla was pumping out over 70  000 model trees while ford was  struggling to pump out 15 000.  now it's not like this production ramp  was easy for tesla given that they  nearly went to bankrupt trying to reach  their production targets what this means  for ford though is that they have yet to  cross the hardest stage of scaling to  make things worse ford is having to do  all this during a historic supply  shortage according to ford's cfo  inflation along with the supply shortage  have completely wiped out the monkey's  profit margin so it's just gonna be even  harder for ford to be scaled than it was  for tesla not to mention it tesla never  had to deal with 200 billion worth of  liabilities either so at this point it  seems like all the cars are stacked  against ford whether it's their balance  sheet or their production capabilities  but one attribute that is working in  their favor is demand ford has over 200  000 lightning reservations and given the  popularity of the f-150 this number will  likely only go up with the time so ford  definitely has the demand to scale their  eva business to the next level and  remain a popular automaker the question  however is will the company survive the  transition  so far i know this video has been rather  negative on ford but they deserve a lot  of credit for many of their choices  at no point has ford tried to half-ass  the transition to evs the company is  literally all in on evs having committed  to invest 50 billion into evs by 2026.  also there doesn't seem to be any doubts  amongst leadership either it seems like  they all unanimously believe that  successfully transitioning to evs is the  only way forward this sort of confidence  and clarity is exactly what every  automaker needs  and honestly in my opinion if any legacy  automaker is going to make it to the  other side it's going to be forward but  with all of that being said it it's not  going to be an easy journey by any means  jack's well intentioned but poorly  executed initiatives from 20 years ago  are still weighing down the company to  this day and honestly there's no easy  way out for ford all they can do is  continue doing what they're already  doing try their best to transition to  evs while also protecting the financial  integrity of their company as much as  possible but only time will tell it if  their ambitious plans actually end up  working out do you think ford will make  it to the other side comment that down  below  also drop a like if you think more  legacy automakers should embrace evs and  of course consider checking out our  discord community such as future video  ideas and the consider subscribing to  more questions that possibly answered  but until then i'm hari and i'll see you  guys on the next one  you

